# Reading notes: CRDTs

Conflict-free replicated data types let replicas accept writes independently and still converge when they exchange state, with no coordination.

Two families:
- **State-based (CvRDT):** replicas send their whole state; merge is a join in a semilattice (commutative, associative, idempotent). Example: G-Counter keeps one counter per replica, merge takes the element-wise max, value is the sum.
- **Operation-based (CmRDT):** replicas send operations; requires reliable causal delivery; ops must commute.

Examples:
- PN-Counter = two G-Counters (increments and decrements).
- OR-Set: each add gets a unique tag; remove deletes the tags it has seen, so a concurrent add wins.
- LWW-Register: last writer wins by timestamp; simple but silently drops a concurrent write.
- Sequence CRDTs (RGA, Logoot, Yjs) for collaborative text.

For our project we decided a full CRDT is overkill: files are mostly edited by one person at a time, so we replay an outbox of operations and, on a real conflict, keep both versions ("conflicted copy") instead of merging text.

Paper: Shapiro et al., "Conflict-free Replicated Data Types", 2011.
